## Additional context

Also, there is no error and the inference runs perfectly fine when I remove the SAGEConv layer from the model. The technical issue is the dimension mismatch between gra (shape: [6,2] )  and adj (shape: [2,36] )  where apparently the gra.shape[0] should be equal to adj.shape[1]. And it indeed works if we use only the first 6 elements of the adj (shape: [2,6] ) which essentially is not what we would intend to do. For my application I need to have multiple inputs of shape ('batch_size',nodes) and edges of the shape ('batch_size', k*nodes) where k indicates the number of k nearest neighbours.

### Notes
I would really appreciate if someone has any leads on this or if anyone has successfully deployed a GNN on ONNX using different input dimensions (where rank of each input <= 2).

After verification, I am inclined towards believing that the issue is with the ONNX exporter which when creating ONNX compatible graph disturbs the shapes of the tensors. ONNX exporter is not yet mature enough to handle Pytorch Geometric's GNN functions like SAGEConv, etc. This needs to be improved.

